WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.077
Start a concurrency review with three
concrete facts: the

2
00:00:04.077 --> 00:00:07.268
authoritative shared state, the invariant
that must survive

3
00:00:07.268 --> 00:00:11.039
each transition, and every writer that can
affect it.

4
00:00:11.039 --> 00:00:13.263
Include request handlers, replicas,
workers,

5
00:00:13.263 --> 00:00:15.487
administrative jobs, and external systems.

6
00:00:15.487 --> 00:00:17.807
The order of statements in one

7
00:00:17.807 --> 00:00:20.708
TypeScript function describes only one
participant's execution.

8
00:00:20.708 --> 00:00:23.608
Asynchronous interleaving is distinct from
CPU parallelism.

9
00:00:23.608 --> 00:00:25.929
One event loop can have overlapping

10
00:00:25.929 --> 00:00:28.443
operations without executing their
JavaScript simultaneously.

11
00:00:28.443 --> 00:00:30.763
Process-local state and distributed shared
state

12
00:00:30.763 --> 00:00:32.890
therefore need different coordination
boundaries.

13
00:00:32.890 --> 00:00:35.308
Await permits other work to progress,

14
00:00:35.308 --> 00:00:37.822
while Promise.all supplies no atomicity
guarantee.

15
00:00:37.822 --> 00:00:40.529
Prefer an atomic conditional mutation when
the

16
00:00:40.529 --> 00:00:43.429
storage layer can express the rule
directly.

17
00:00:43.429 --> 00:00:45.750
Check its result and preserve database

18
00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:47.877
constraints that protect the invariant.

19
00:00:47.877 --> 00:00:51.841
Application validation improves feedback
and expresses business rules, but an

20
00:00:51.841 --> 00:00:55.515
earlier check does not enforce a later
shared-state transition.

21
00:00:55.515 --> 00:00:58.706
Use transactions when related changes must
succeed together,

22
00:00:58.706 --> 00:01:01.800
then verify isolation, locking, and
predicate coverage.

23
00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:04.701
A transaction is not automatic serial
execution.

24
00:01:04.701 --> 00:01:07.021
Keep protected work focused and avoid

25
00:01:07.021 --> 00:01:09.922
slow remote calls while locks are held.

26
00:01:09.922 --> 00:01:11.952
Optimistic concurrency detects stale
versions;

27
00:01:11.952 --> 00:01:14.079
pessimistic locking limits conflicting
access.

28
00:01:14.079 --> 00:01:18.914
Comparing a version and advancing it must
occur atomically with the write.

29
00:01:18.914 --> 00:01:21.041
Treat retries, queue redelivery, and

30
00:01:21.041 --> 00:01:23.168
duplicate requests as expected behavior.

31
00:01:23.168 --> 00:01:25.489
Idempotency preserves one logical outcome
across

32
00:01:25.489 --> 00:01:28.486
attempts, whereas mutual exclusion
separates overlapping work.

33
00:01:28.486 --> 00:01:31.967
Retrying an operation is safe only when
replay and

34
00:01:31.967 --> 00:01:35.738
recovery semantics are defined, including
effects in external systems.

35
00:01:35.738 --> 00:01:38.928
Test meaningful overlap and inspect
authoritative final state,

36
00:01:38.928 --> 00:01:42.602
including the absence of partial effects
from rejected attempts.

37
00:01:42.602 --> 00:01:47.147
Types can document states and versions,
but runtime guarantees enforce them.

38
00:01:47.147 --> 00:01:50.434
Authorization remains a separate
requirement for every transition.

39
00:01:50.434 --> 00:01:54.302
Concurrency correctness comes from
protecting the invariant at the layer

40
00:01:54.302 --> 00:01:57.879
where competing operations actually meet,
not from assuming the

41
00:01:57.879 --> 00:02:01.940
operations will happen in the order the
source code suggests.

